{
  "passage": "Some foods taste sour because they contain acids. Lemon juice contains citric acid, curd contains lactic acid, and vinegar contains acetic acid. Substances that taste bitter and feel soapy, such as baking soda, are called bases. Tasting is not a safe way to test a substance, so we use indicators. An indicator changes its colour when it is added to an acid or a base. Litmus is a natural indicator obtained from lichens. Blue litmus paper turns red in an acid, while red litmus paper turns blue in a base. Turmeric is another natural indicator found in every Indian kitchen. A turmeric stain on a white cloth turns red when soap, which is a base, is rubbed on it, and it turns yellow again when washed with plenty of water. China rose petals give a solution that turns dark pink in an acid and green in a base. When an acid is mixed with a base, they cancel each other's effect. This reaction is called neutralisation, and it produces a salt and water, along with heat. Neutralisation is useful in daily life. An ant bite injects formic acid into the skin, and the pain can be relieved by rubbing on a mild base such as moist baking soda. Farmers add quicklime or slaked lime to soil that is too acidic.",
